---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Statement from '../components/Statement.astro';
import { business, plan, money } from '../data/business';

const title = 'How it works — Wardith';
const description =
  'How Wardith gets your business found by AI assistants: a £250 audit of where you stand, a one-off Foundation setup, and an ongoing monthly plan to keep you visible.';

const audit = plan('audit');
const foundation = plan('foundation');
const maintain = plan('maintain');

const howToSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: business.serviceName,
  serviceType: business.serviceType,
  provider: { '@id': new URL('/#organization', Astro.site).href },
  areaServed: business.areaServed,
  description:
    'Wardith audits how AI assistants such as ChatGPT, Google, Copilot and Perplexity currently answer questions about a business, fixes what is blocking the business from being found, and maintains that visibility month to month.',
};
---

<Base title={title} description={description} schema={[howToSchema]}>
  <section class="hero hero--rules">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>How does Wardith get you found?</h1>
      <p class="lede" data-reveal>
        Three stages, in order: find out where you stand, fix what's in the way, then keep you
        visible as the AI systems change.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      {/* The map, before the territory. The three stages below say what each
          one contains; this says what shape the whole thing is — and, more to
          the point, that it is allowed to end at any rung. That was previously
          only discoverable from the closing statement, several hundred words
          past the point where a reader decides whether this is a service or a
          funnel. Deliberately carries no prices: the moment it does, it stops
          being a diagram of the process and becomes a plan comparison. */}
      <div class="split" style="margin-bottom: 3.5rem;">
        <div data-reveal>
          <h2>Three stages, and the way out of each one</h2>
        </div>
        <div data-reveal>
          <ol class="ladder">
            <li class="rung">
              <p class="rung-name">Audit</p>
              <p class="rung-say">Find out where you stand today.</p>
              <p class="rung-exit">
                <b>It can end here.</b> If there's nothing meaningful to fix, the report says so and
                we don't sell you the next stage.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li class="rung">
              <p class="rung-name">Foundation</p>
              <p class="rung-say">Fix what the audit found, on the website you already have.</p>
              <p class="rung-exit">
                <b>It can end here too.</b> Foundation is a one-off piece of work with a fixed
                scope, and nothing about it obliges you to take a monthly plan.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li class="rung">
              <p class="rung-name">Ongoing</p>
              <p class="rung-say">Keep checking, as the assistants and their sources change.</p>
              <p class="rung-exit">
                <b>And it can end whenever you like.</b> Monthly, no minimum term.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ol class="stages">
        <li class="stage" data-reveal>
          <div class="stage-head">
            <h3>The audit</h3>
            <span class="stage-price">{money(audit.price)} · one-off</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stage-body">
            <p>
              We start by asking the major AI assistants the questions your customers would ask —
              in your service area, in your specialism — and recording exactly what they say. We
              check whether they know your business exists, what they believe about you, whether
              it's accurate, and who they recommend instead of you.
            </p>
            <p>
              Then we look at why. We check what the AI crawlers can and can't read on your
              website, and whether anything on it answers the questions being asked. We also check
              the places outside your website that these systems draw on, because that is where
              most of the fixable problems turn out to be: your Google Business Profile, Bing
              Places, Companies House if you're a company, whichever professional register applies
              to your trade, the main directories, and your LinkedIn and Facebook pages. We record
              your name, address and phone exactly as each one has them — an old address on a
              listing nobody has looked at in five years is the single most common finding, and
              almost nobody knows it's there.
            </p>
            <p>
              You get a short written report in plain English: where you show up today, what's
              blocking you, and what we'd do about it. If you're already in good shape, the report
              says so and we don't try to sell you the next stage.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li class="stage" data-reveal>
          <div class="stage-head">
            <h3>The Foundation</h3>
            <span class="stage-price">{money(foundation.price)} · one-off</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stage-body">
            <p>
              The audit tells us what's wrong. The Foundation is the fixed-scope work that
              addresses the underlying issues we're equipped to fix. Working on your existing
              website — we don't rebuild websites — it does four things:
            </p>
            <ul class="ticks">
              <li>
                <strong>Makes your website reachable and readable.</strong> AI crawlers are allowed
                in, and your pages can be read as plain text rather than only as a rendered app.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Makes the facts about your business unambiguous.</strong> Who you are, what
                you do, where you operate and what you charge, added as structured data so those
                facts are explicitly machine-readable rather than left to be inferred from the
                layout of the page.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Stops your own information contradicting itself.</strong> Name, services,
                location and contact details made consistent everywhere they appear online, so
                nothing has to decide which version of you to believe.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Fills the two gaps that cost you most.</strong> Two permanent pages that
                directly answer questions your customers ask, in language an assistant can quote.
                Your audit decides which two — they'll be the questions you're most visibly missing
                from.
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              That's the whole of it, and the scope is deliberately fixed rather than open: you know
              before you start exactly what you're buying. Not every audit leads here. If the
              biggest thing in your way sits outside those four — as it did when we ran the audit on
              ourselves — we'll tell you that instead of quietly adding it to the bill.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li class="stage" data-reveal>
          <div class="stage-head">
            <h3>Ongoing</h3>
            <span class="stage-price">from {money(maintain.price)} · per month</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stage-body">
            <p>
              AI systems, the search indexes behind them and the sources they draw on all change
              constantly, so visibility is something you maintain rather than something you buy
              once. Once the foundations are in place we keep testing: where you appear, what has
              changed since last month, where competitors have gained ground, and which questions
              you're still missing from. The higher plans go further than watching — they close the
              gaps as they're found. Which plan does what is on the
              <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--invert">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div data-reveal>
        <h2>What don't we do?</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div data-reveal>
          <p>
            <strong>We don't guarantee recommendations.</strong> Nobody can responsibly guarantee a
            particular recommendation, because the answer is produced by the AI system rather than
            by whoever is working on your business. What we do is put the things we can observe and
            influence in place — and show you, in plain before-and-after terms, how you're showing
            up.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>We don't build websites.</strong> The work happens on the site you already have.
            If your site has problems beyond our scope, the audit will say so honestly.
          </p>
          {/* Shortened 2026-08-09 when the diagram above went in. The two
              sentences that came out — audit and Foundation are one-offs,
              monthly has no minimum term — are now the exits on the ladder,
              and saying them twice on one page made the diagram look like
              decoration rather than the place that fact lives. What is left is
              the part the diagram can't carry: how you actually stop. */}
          <p>
            <strong>We don't do lock-ins.</strong> Tell us before your next payment date and there
            won't be one.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Statement
        cite={`The audit costs ${money(audit.price)}, is a one-off, and tells you where you appear today — including the awkward finding that you might not need us.`}
      >
        Every stage has to earn <em>the next one.</em>
      </Statement>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
